# Oakhaven (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): the Rainwood. Slow, rooted, warm; trees, roots, bark and shade.
# Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar oakhaven
greet = Shade on you | Good shade
greet.broad = Shade an' shelter
farewell = Root well | Shade keep you
agree = Rooted true | Aye, that
refuse = That won't take root
surprise = Leaf and limb | Well, branch me
annoyed = Blight it | Knots and knots
very = proper
good = good bark | sound wood
bad = rotten | windthrown
worthless = deadwood
luck = good growing weather
child = sapling
pet!! = acorn | my sapling
tired = felled
nonsense = sawdust
tag = , mm?
oath! = Bark and branch | Old Oak | Root and rain
home = under my own roof-beam
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = old root | friend
address[other.stage=young] = sapling | young 'un
bed = my pallet | the bough-bed
cold = nippy | cold as a stump
dear! = my oak | my heartwood | love
drunk = sap-soaked | mellow
hot = close under the leaves | sultry
hungry = hollow as a log | hungry
hurt = barked | sore
little = a twig | a little
money = coin | acorns
rain = drip | leaf-rain
robbery = Rank robbery, that | Stripped bare, like beetles had been at it
saying! = Slow grows the oak | Every tree falls in its season | Roots first, then branches
thanks = Thank you kindly | Bless you
work = the cutting | the work
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = pay | coin
boss = the old oak | the master
busy = at it root and branch | busy
slow = slow as sap | quiet
cheated = barked | done
mum! = Mam | Mother
dad! = Da | Father
gran = Granny | Old Mother
grandad = Grandsire | Old Father
supper = supper | the pot
toffs = the city folk | the polished lot
busybody = magpie | gossip
fool = you great stump | you knot-head
daft = woodwormed | daft
toast = Deep roots | Good shade | Sap rise
drink = a cup of perry | a jar
fog = mist in the trees | fog
wind = wind in the tops | a blow
ale = acorn ale | ale
thief = nut-robber | thief
bandits = wood-robbers | bandits
grave = under the roots | the ground
grub = victuals | grub
snug = snug as a squirrel | cosy
poorly = drooping | poorly
remedy = willow bark | a moss poultice | elder syrup
olddays = the old growth | days gone by
blessing = Shade keep you | Root well | Good growing to you
bless = Shade keep you | Root and keep you
someday = some green year | one day
far = through the wood and out | a long walk
stranger = outlander | stranger
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = Take your time under my roof. | Have a look, old root. | Good wood, good goods.
shop_dear = Things grow slow, and cost to match. | Dear as heartwood. | The woods don't give it cheap.
shop_sold = Root well with it. | There, it'll last. | Made to keep.
shop_bye = Shade keep you. | Root well. | Come back by the old oak.
shop_bare = Picked clean. | Nothing on the bough just now. | Bare as a felled stump.
talk_open = What's on your mind, old root? | Sit a while. What is it? | Speak slow, I'm listening.
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There's work, old root, if you'll take it. | I could use a hand under these boughs. | Something needs doing.
work_taken = Good. Root well and get on. | Good. Steady does it. | Settled, then.
work_paid = Here, as promised, old root. | Paid, and shade on you. | Fairly done. There you are.
work_back = No harm done. Another will take it. | Fair enough. It'll grow back. | Better said now, old root.
work_none = Nothing going, old root. | No work under my roof today. | Nothing needs doing.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Sit a while, old root. A bed or a bite? | Come in under the beams. What'll it be? | Bed, board or a cup?
inn_bed = Upstairs, under the old beams. There's a chest for your things. | Your bed's upstairs. Root well tonight.
watch_greet = The watch, old root. Speak slow. | Keep the peace and we'll grow along fine.
church_greet = Shade on you. Come in. | Sit in the shade a while. All are welcome.
learn_offer = It's a trade that grows on you. | Slow work, but it lasts.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... shut, old root... come back at sunup. | Zzz... let a body sleep. Morning.
news_none = Quiet under the trees. | Nothing's stirred lately. | Not a leaf turned.
gossip_none = Magpie talk. I keep out of it. | Nothing I'd repeat, old root.
trouble_none = Nothing that won't grow out. | Nothing, old root.
trouble_thanks = Shade on you. I won't forget it. | You've a good root in you. Thank you.
way_ask = Where are you headed, old root? | Lost among the trees?
job_busy = This and that, slow and steady. | I keep busy, old root.
work_declined = Suit yourself, old root. | Another season, then.
inn_rest = Sit a while under the beams. A bed upstairs rests you properly. | Take the bench by the fire, old root.
watch_noted = Noted. We'll root them out. | Good. The watch thanks you, old root.
watch_bye = Keep the peace, old root. | Move along, then.
learn_taken = Good. Be here at sunup, and stay near me while I work. | Then you're my sapling. Stay close.
receiver_ask = Something for me, old root? | For me? Sit a while.
inn_helper = I only help here, old root. Ask #here.innkeeper# for a bed. | #here.innkeeper# keeps the house. I carry the cups.

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): Oakhaven's hedge, account, preclose, excuse and the rest (plain.grammar
# says what each is). Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
hedge = Well, mm | Slow, now | Let me chew on it, slow
account = I've my own roots to tend | It's not the season for it
ack = Mm, is that so | So it grows
pivot = That's a branch off the same tree. | Which puts me in mind, mm.
anyway = Anyway, let that lie. | Enough of that old wood.
later = in good time | by and by
resume = To the root of it | Now, the root of it
preclose = Well. Time I rooted off | Still. The hour's growing on
excuse = The hour's growing on | I've roots to tend
knew = I'd heard, mm | That's old leaf-fall
sympathy = That's a heavy bough | A hard winter for you
concede = You may be right, mm | I'll give you that branch
insist = I'm rooted on it | Mark me, old root
warn = Mind the roots underfoot | Have a care in the trees
welcome_back = Back under the same tree | You again, old root
welcome = Come under the branches | There's shade for three
sorry = I spoke too quick, mm | No offence, old root
forgive = Let it lie like leaves | No harm, mm
deflect = No bother, mm | Any time, old root
no_matter = In its season, then | No matter, mm
go_ahead = Ask away, mm | Go on, old root
put_off = Not this season | In good time, mm
vent = Between us and the old oak | Truth to the root
reassure = It'll grow right | Roots hold, mm
congrats = Good growing, that | Well rooted
demur = That won't take root | I'd not lean that way
doubt = Hollow, that sounds | Show me the roots first
listen = Listen, mm | Hark, now

# Phase 8 (doc 84 §33): the shared lines' words, as Oakhaven says them. Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
marvel = Well, mm, there's new leaf | Branch me, now | Mm, that's a new acorn
pity = A bough broke, that | That's a cruel wind, mm
gladnews = That's good sap rising | New leaf, that, mm
uneasy = There's a wind in the branches | I don't like the creak of that
grumble = Knots everywhere, mm | Hard bark, all of it
notsobad = The root's sound yet | I've seen trees bend worse
dunno = That's all the leaves said | No more on that branch, mm
hearsay = Leaf-talk, mm, that's all | It came on the wind
howso = Slow, now. How so | How's that, mm
rest = Root deep, and rest | The old oak keep the soul
# Phase 8, session 2 (doc 84 §33.3): the other owed moves' words, as Oakhaven says them. Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
funny = Ha, you'll shake my leaves | That's a merry branch, mm
cheek = Hark at the sapling | Says the acorn
modest = Slow growth, nothing more | The roots did it, mm
secret = Down in the roots | Not a whisper in the leaves
advise = Take it from an old root | Grow it slow, mm
helpout = Need a branch to lean on | Want an extra paw, mm
wellsaid = Sound timber, that | That's heartwood sense
poorshow = That's rotten wood | Dead branch, that
fine = Still standing, mm | Rooted, as ever
planless = Slow, as the trees go | Whatever the season says
pardon = I only said, mm | What I said, old root, was
tale = That's an old-wood tale | One for under the branches, that
# Greetings and closings for rules/shared8_turns.talk (plain.grammar's s8_* say who; here, the dialect's own).
s8_hello = Good shade, old root | Shade on you, mm | Ah, old root | Well, mm, hello
s8_okay = Mm, go on, then | Slow, now. Off you go | Root well, then | Go on, old root
s8_bye = Root well, old root | Good shade, mm | Step over the roots | Go slow, mm
s8_byeback = And good shade to you | Root well, mm | Step over the roots, now | Slow, now, mm
s8_night = Sleep like an old root | Mind the roots, now | Rest under the green, mm | Sound sleep, mm
s8_nightback = Root deep tonight, mm | And sound sleep to you | Rest under the green
s8_nevermind = In its season, then | Mm. No matter | Let it grow

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7): plain's short answers ("Good.", "Always.", "It is.") as Oakhaven says them.
# Drafts, to be tuned by ear; one left out falls back to plain's words.
bc_good = Good bark. | Sound wood, that.
bc_always = Always, root and branch. | Same as the old oak.
bc_thank_you = Thank you kindly. | Thank you, from the root.
bc_it_is = It is, mm. | Proper, it is.
bc_ha = Ha, mm. | Ha. That's a good knot.
bc_i_will = I will, in my own time. | I will, mm.
bc_very = Proper. | Proper so.
bc_never = Never, root nor branch. | Never in a hundred rings.
bc_probably = Likely, mm. | Like as not.
bc_we_will_see = We'll see what grows. | Time'll tell, mm.
bc_maybe = Maybe, mm. | Happen so.
bc_me_too = Same tree. | Me too, mm.
bc_it_does = It does, mm. | Proper, it does.
bc_again = Again. Knots and knots. | Again, round as a ring.
bc_lovely = Proper lovely. | Lovely as new leaf.
bc_every_year = Every year, like leaf-fall. | Every year, ring on ring.
bc_wise = Wise as an old oak. | Old-root wise.
bc_i_know = I know, mm. | Know it in my roots.
bc_so_they_say = So the old roots say. | So they say, mm.
bc_again_q = Again, mm? | Again? Knots and knots.
bc_of_course = Of course, old root. | Course, mm.
bc_about_time = About time, mm. | Slow as sap, but there.
bc_mine_too = Mine too, mm. | Same tree.
bc_ill_try = I'll try, slow and sure. | I'll try, mm.
bc_nor_me = Nor me, mm. | Nor me, old root.
bc_they_do = They do, mm. | That they do, proper.
bc_poor_soul = Poor soul, mm. | Poor old root.
bc_i_wont = Not a leaf of it. | I won't, mm.
bc_every_one = Every leaf on the tree. | Every one, mm.
bc_not_me = Not this old root. | Not me, mm.
bc_very_good = Proper good. | Good bark, that.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then, mm. | Go on, take root.
bc_barely = Barely, by a twig. | Barely, mm.
bc_lucky_you = Lucky old root. | Lucky you, mm.
bc_every_night = Every night, mm. | Every night, like the owls.
bc_nobody = Not a soul, mm. | Nobody, root nor branch.
bc_for_once = For once, mm. | Once in a hundred rings.
bc_i_hope_so = I hope so, mm. | Here's hoping, root and branch.
bc_exactly = Rooted true. | Exactly, mm.
bc_everything = Root and branch. | The whole tree.
bc_true = True, mm. | Rooted true.
bc_mostly = Mostly, mm. | Mostly, root and branch.
bc_gladly = Gladly, mm. | Gladly, old root.
bc_fair_enough = Fair enough, mm. | Rooted fair.
bc_sorry = Sorry, old root.
bc_every_time = Every time, mm. | Every season, every time.
bc_very_bad = Proper rotten. | Rotten to the root.
bc_fair = Fair, mm. | Fair and sound.
bc_bless_you = Shade bless you. | Bless you, mm.
bc_always_is = Always is, mm. | Always was, always is.
bc_right = Right, mm. | Right enough.
bc_lucky = Lucky, mm. | Good growing, that.
bc_bad_luck = Rotten as a stump, that. | Windthrown luck.
bc_why_q = Why, mm? | Why's that, old root?
bc_true_enough = Rooted true. | True enough, mm.
bc_they_always_do = They always do, mm. | Always do, root and branch.
bc_every_day = Every day, mm. | Every day, root to branch.
bc_very_busy = At it root and branch. | Proper busy.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry, old root. | I'm proper sorry.
bc_so_do_i = So do I, mm. | Same tree.
bc_clever = Clever, mm. | Clever as a squirrel.
bc_too_late = Too late. The tree's down. | Too late, mm.
bc_go_on = Go on, mm. | Go on, old root.
bc_we_will = We will, mm. | That we will, in time.
bc_they_are = They are, mm. | That they are, proper.
bc_so_am_i = So am I, mm. | Same tree.
bc_let_them = Let them, mm. | Let them grow how they will.
bc_hope_so = Hope so, mm. | Hope it takes root.
bc_already = Already, mm. | Long since, old root.
bc_i_might = I might, mm. | I might, in my own time.
bc_the_best = Best in the wood. | The best, mm.
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to, mm. | Somebody has to tend the roots.
bc_nothing = Nothing, mm. | Not a leaf.
bc_and_q = And, mm? | And then, old root?
bc_who_q = Who, mm? | Who's that, old root?
bc_it_was = It was, mm. | That it was, proper.
